# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in. Every environment variable the app
# reads must be documented here with a safe placeholder — never a real value.
# A setting that no code reads does not belong here: it reads as a control
# and is a decoration, and two of them sat in this file for three releases.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED. Every setting has a command-line flag or a
# sensible answer the tool works out for itself, and this file exists so an
# archive you convert more than once does not need them typed twice:
#
#     fpx-converter scan ./photos
#     fpx-converter convert --dest ./converted
#
# .env is looked for in the current directory first, then in your config
# directory (%APPDATA%\fpx-converter on Windows, ~/.config/fpx-converter
# elsewhere), then beside the package. The first one found wins, and
# --env-file overrides the search. Real environment variables beat the file.
#
# Real paths are deliberately NOT in this file: it is committed to the repo,
# and a source path identifies somebody's personal photo archive. Put yours
# in a local .env, which is gitignored.

# --- Paths -------------------------------------------------------------
# The source archive. READ-ONLY: the pipeline never writes, moves, renames,
# or deletes anything under this path. Point it at the backup tree that
# holds the .fpx files. `scan /path/to/photos` says the same thing for one
# run; this is the convenience for saying it once.
FPX_SOURCE_ROOT=C:\path\to\backups\CDDVD

# Where the manifest, the ingested .fpx store and the default output/ live.
# Unset means the current directory — or the checkout, when the tool is run
# from a clone of its own repository. The same thing as --work-dir.
# FPX_WORK_DIR=C:\path\to\fpx-work

# Where derivatives are written (archive/, sharing/, report/, and the logs).
# The default for `convert --dest` and `gallery --dest`.
# Expect roughly 2.5 GB for a 1,265-file corpus. Keep this path SHORT --
# Windows long-path support is disabled on the dev machine and deep paths
# corrupt writes. If it sits inside a cloud-synced folder, pin it
# "always keep on this device" first, or sync will upload the whole archive.
FPX_OUTPUT_ROOT=C:\path\to\fpx-output

# --- External tools ----------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path to the ExifTool executable. Leave unset to resolve it from
# PATH, which is what the standard install gives you:
#   Windows  winget install --id OliverBetz.ExifTool
#   macOS    brew install exiftool
#   Linux    apt install libimage-exiftool-perl
# `convert` refuses to start without it rather than writing every image and
# then reporting every one of them failed. The same thing as --exiftool.
# FPX_EXIFTOOL=C:\path\to\ExifTool.exe

# --- Behaviour ---------------------------------------------------------
# IANA time zone the collection was shot in. This does NOT convert stored
# timestamps — they are already local wall-clock time (see docs/DATES.md).
# It selects the EXIF OffsetTimeOriginal / OffsetTimeDigitized values that
# get written, so modern apps can compute the absolute instant.
#
# Unset means this machine's own time zone. Set it when the photographs were
# taken somewhere other than where you are converting them. The same thing as
# --timezone.
# FPX_DEFAULT_TZ=America/Chicago

# Optional JSON map of album name -> IANA time zone, for albums shot away
# from home. Overrides FPX_DEFAULT_TZ for those albums only.
# FPX_TZ_OVERRIDES={"Some Trip":"America/New_York"}

# Source folders whose names say nothing about their contents, so their photos
# are filed under <year>/<year> <Month> instead of keeping the folder name.
# `layout.NON_DESCRIPTIVE_ALBUMS` already covers names that are generic in any
# archive ("untitled", "new folder", "misc"). This is for names specific to
# yours -- and since folder names are personal content, they belong here and
# not in the repository. JSON list of strings, matched case-insensitively.
# FPX_NON_DESCRIPTIVE_ALBUMS=["SomeDumpFolder", "scan batch 3"]

# Albums whose folder name LOOKS day-precise but is not. A holiday name
# resolves to a calendar day, but a folder named for one may hold the whole
# season around it -- the eve, the day after, the week either side -- and only
# the person who made the folder knows which. Listing it here demotes the name
# to its year: the album still files and sorts under that year, and nothing is
# written to DateTimeOriginal. The demotion is one-way; it can take a claim
# away, never add one. Album names are personal content, so they live here.
# FPX_COARSE_ALBUMS=["Midwinter Feast 1994", "Summer Trip 1995"]

# FPX_LOG_LEVEL and FPX_WORKERS were documented here and read by no code at
# all — there is no log-level control and the batch engine is single-process
# on purpose. They are deleted rather than implemented: a setting nobody
# reads is worse than a missing one, because it looks like it works.
